<task id="product-research" label="Step 1: Product Research">
## Product Research — URL + Notes → Client Profile

**Use the `product-research` skill** to turn the URL and natural-language notes into the structured profile every later step consumes.

```bash
uv run --project skills/product-research/runtime \
  skills/product-research/runtime/research_product.py \
  --url "{websiteUrl from @product-info}" \
  --notes "{productNotes from @product-info}" \
  -o product_profile_tmp.json
```

Read `brand_folder` from the output, create the folder, and move the profile in:

```bash
BRAND_FOLDER=$(python3 -c "import json; print(json.load(open('product_profile_tmp.json'))['brand_folder'])")
mkdir -p "$BRAND_FOLDER/thumbnails"
mv product_profile_tmp.json "$BRAND_FOLDER/product_profile.json"
```

All subsequent outputs go into `{brandFolder}/`. The profile provides `client_name`, `client_description`, `niche_label`, `keyword_seeds`, and `known_competitor_candidates` for the steps below.

**Output:** `{brandFolder}/product_profile.json`
</task>

<task id="competitor-research" label="Step 2: Competitor Research">
## Discover and Verify True Competitors

**Use the `competitor-research` skill**, seeded with the candidates from the product profile:

```bash
uv run --project skills/competitor-research/runtime \
  skills/competitor-research/runtime/research_competitors.py \
  --client "{client_name from product_profile.json}" \
  --description "{client_description from product_profile.json}" \
  --website "{websiteUrl from @product-info}" \
  --competitors "{comma-separated names from product_profile.json → known_competitor_candidates}" \
  --max-competitors 12 \
  --tiktok-presence \
  --report \
  -o {brandFolder}/competitors_research.json
```

`--tiktok-presence` also produces `{brandFolder}/competitors_tiktok_presence.json` and `--report` writes `{brandFolder}/competitor_report.md`.

Then derive `{brandFolder}/competitors_confirmed.json` from the Tier 1 + Tier 2 competitors (same shape as the per-platform workflows use). In an interactive session, pause at @review-competitors first; in an autonomous run, confirm Tier 1 + Tier 2 as-is and note that in the file.

**Output:** `{brandFolder}/competitors_research.json`, `competitors_confirmed.json`, `competitors_tiktok_presence.json`, `competitor_report.md`
</task>

<break id="review-competitors" label="Review Competitor List (optional)">
Review `{brandFolder}/competitors_research.json`. Keep / remove / add competitors, then continue. Skip this break when running autonomously — Tier 1 + Tier 2 are confirmed automatically.
</break>

<task id="keyword-research" label="Step 3: Keyword Research (all platforms)">
## Generate Platform-Native Keywords

Reuse the platform keyword skills where they exist, seeded from `product_profile.json → keyword_seeds`:

```bash
# TikTok keywords (top 20)
uv run --project skills/tiktok-keyword-research/runtime \
  skills/tiktok-keyword-research/runtime/research_keywords.py \
  --client "{client_name}" --description "{client_description}" \
  --website "{websiteUrl}" \
  --competitors "{comma-separated confirmed competitor names}" \
  --max-keywords 20 \
  -o {brandFolder}/keywords_tiktok.json

# Instagram keywords (top 50: competitor_keywords for Meta Ads + top_keywords for Reels)
uv run --project skills/instagram-keyword-research/runtime \
  skills/instagram-keyword-research/runtime/research_keywords.py \
  --client "{client_name}" --description "{client_description}" \
  --website "{websiteUrl}" \
  --competitors "{comma-separated confirmed competitor names}" \
  --max-keywords 50 \
  -o {brandFolder}/keywords_instagram.json
```

For YouTube, derive `{brandFolder}/keywords_youtube.json` from the TikTok keywords + `keyword_seeds`, following the YouTube-native guidance in `youtube-shorts-research.anyt` (question-based queries, "shorts" suffix variants, competitor names).

**App products (`product_profile.json → is_app`):** when the client is an app, use "xxx app" keyword variants everywhere organic search happens — "spark app", "mint finance app", "best dating apps" — plain common-word app names can be ambiguous in social search. The keyword skills apply this automatically; carry it into the YouTube keyword derivation too. (Meta Ads Library `--advertiser` names stay the exact brand name.)

**Split keywords into four sets, and browse all four.** Theme terms surface
the broad space, but product and brand-owned terms are what fill an on-category
deck and expose the strongest official posts:

- **Theme set:** broad category language for context only.
- **Product set:** client and competitor product/model names plus high-intent
  actions: `review`, `vs`, `comparison`, `sizing`, `fitting`, `setup`,
  `maintenance`, `repair`, `test`, `best X for Y`. For apps, include explicit
  `app`, `app review`, `apps like X`, and `X alternative` variants.
- **Brand-owned set:** verified account/channel names paired with their own
  products and models, e.g. `{brand} {model}`, `{brand} setup`, `{brand}
  comparison`, and `{brand} review`.
- **Content-type set:** entity-qualified phrases for missing lanes: `official`,
  `founder story`, `behind the scenes`, `ad`, `commercial`, `product demo`,
  `tutorial`, `how it works`, `comparison`, `honest review`, `customer story`,
  `before after`, and `results`. Never search a bare format phrase without a
  client, competitor, product, model, or precise use-case anchor.

Store the latter two as `keywords_{platform}_products.json` and
`keywords_{platform}_brands.json`. Step 4 runs the sweeps separately and retains
their query provenance. Never let a high-performing theme result displace a
lower-reach video that actually demonstrates a product decision or service.

Derive a third **branded partnership set** for the client plus every confirmed
Tier 1 and Tier 2 brand. It is a broad creator-candidate sweep, not a disclosure
filter: likely UGC often uses only the product name or brand hashtag. For each
brand include:

- the exact brand name and its common product-name variant
- the compact brand hashtag, stripping spaces and punctuation only when an
  official hashtag is not already visible (`Wispr Flow` -> `#WisprFlow`)
- non-disclosure product-use queries: `{brand} review`, `{brand} demo`,
  `{brand} tutorial`, `{brand} app`, plus one brand-specific use case
- `{brand} ad`, `{brand} sponsored`, `{brand} partner`, `{brand} partnership`,
  `{brand} collaboration`, `partnered with {brand}`, and `{brand} ambassador`
- platform-native disclosure combinations such as `#{compact_brand} #ad` and
  `#{compact_brand} #sponsored`

Store these separately as `keywords_{platform}_partnerships.json`. For every
brand, run the exact name, hashtag, and at least two product-use queries even if
the disclosure queries return results. Do not merge candidates into organic
discovery before provenance is recorded: the query starts attribution, while
video and creator-profile evidence determine whether the post belongs on the
brand page or the organic page.

When this produces more than 12 queries, browse one brand per invocation and
merge the JSON pools. A single large batch can hit a platform timeout before the
later competitors run, which creates false zeros in `keyword_breakdown`.

**Output:** `{brandFolder}/keywords_tiktok.json`, `keywords_instagram.json`,
`keywords_youtube.json`, and `keywords_{platform}_partnerships.json`
</task>

<task id="browse-tiktok" label="Step 4a: Browse TikTok">
## Browse TikTok with Keywords

Same flow as `tiktok-research.anyt` Step 4 (single round — hashtag expansion is a follow-up workflow):

```bash
uv run --project skills/browse-tiktok-research/runtime \
  skills/browse-tiktok-research/runtime/browse.py \
  {product queries first, then theme queries; each in quotes} \
  --sort-by likes \
  --time-range {timeRange from @product-info} \
  -n 10 \
  --max-time 300 \
  --no-details \
  -o {brandFolder}/browse_tiktok.json
```

Run the branded partnership set separately. Do not filter it to disclosure
terms: plain brand, hashtag, review, demo, and tutorial queries are first-class
inputs. Keep Phase 2 details enabled so the result includes current views, likes,
follower count, caption, and uploader signals needed to distinguish KOCs/KOLs
and evaluate distribution:

```bash
uv run --project skills/browse-tiktok-research/runtime \
  skills/browse-tiktok-research/runtime/browse.py \
  {queries from keywords_tiktok_partnerships.json, each in quotes} \
  --sort-by likes \
  --time-range {timeRange from @product-info} \
  -n 20 \
  --max-time 300 \
  -o {brandFolder}/browse_tiktok_partnerships.json
```

Review `summary.keyword_breakdown` for every partnership query. A brand hashtag
returning zero is a retry/fallback target, not proof the brand has no creator
program. Resolve TikTok share URLs to permanent `@handle/video/{id}` URLs before
curation and preserve the original URL as source evidence. For shortlisted
creator posts, open `@handle` and record the public bio plus outbound link. A
brand name/domain or `ambassador`/`partner` claim is relationship evidence; a
generic Linktree URL is not unless its destination names or links the brand.

Note: TikTok search results show **likes**, not views. If the browse hits a login wall, use web search with `site:tiktok.com` queries and TikTok oEmbed metadata; record `"source": "search-fallback"` in the JSON. Do not request a search-model API key.

Then run the verified brand-owned queries separately and write
`browse_tiktok_brands.json`. Search results show **likes**, not views; do not
silently relabel the metric.

**Output:** `{brandFolder}/browse_tiktok.json`,
`{brandFolder}/browse_tiktok_brands.json`,
`{brandFolder}/browse_tiktok_partnerships.json`, and summaries

Steps 4a-4d are independent — run all four in parallel via `runtime/run_phase.py run --bg` (phases `platform-tiktok`, `platform-instagram`, `platform-meta-ads`, `platform-youtube`), then observe them with `runtime/run_phase.py status --wait --max-wait 45`. If jobs remain, update the user and repeat the bounded status slice. Sequential platform browsing is a defect, not a style choice; fall back to it only when a host cannot keep detached processes alive.
</task>

<task id="browse-instagram" label="Step 4b: Browse Instagram Reels">
## Browse Instagram Reels

Run product queries first for organic creators. Run verified brand handles and
brand+product queries separately for official content:

```bash
uv run --project skills/browse-instagram-reels/runtime \
  skills/browse-instagram-reels/runtime/browse.py \
  {product queries from keywords_instagram_products.json} \
  -n 10 \
  --min-views 50000 \
  -o {brandFolder}/browse_instagram.json
```

Run `keywords_instagram_partnerships.json` as a separate browse with the same
runtime and write `{brandFolder}/browse_instagram_partnerships.json`. Search both
plain brand names and brand hashtags, plus review/demo/tutorial variants. On
accepted Reels retain the caption, hashtags, tagged brand handle, creator handle,
and any paid-partnership label or disclosure language. Inspect the creator bio
and link-in-bio for the named brand/domain or an ambassador/partner claim. A
hashtag alone opens the candidate; it neither proves payment nor disqualifies
the Reel from further analysis.

If Instagram requires login and no session is available — or the browse returns mostly low-view content — use the skill's **login-less discovery script**, which searches indexed reels, reads real og-tag metrics, and **self-expands with hashtags mined from accepted reels** until enough high-engagement content qualifies:

```bash
uv run --project skills/browse-instagram-reels/runtime \
  skills/browse-instagram-reels/runtime/discover_reels.py \
  {top_keywords} --min-likes 10000 --target 8 --rounds 3 \
  -o {brandFolder}/browse_instagram_discovered.json
```

Try `--min-likes 50000` first; drop to `10000` only if the relevant product
pool is thin. This floor applies only to organic creators. For official content,
inspect each verified account grid and run brand+product searches; collect at
least 10 on-category candidates before selecting five when the platform exposes
enough. Merge those with attributable creator placements from the partnership
sweep. Keep owned posts regardless of engagement when they add distinct
positioning evidence, but prefer stronger relevant partnership UGC when it
better represents the brand's distribution strategy. Use public verified pages
as the last resort and preserve source labels.

**Output:** `{brandFolder}/browse_instagram.json`,
`{brandFolder}/browse_instagram_brands.json`,
`{brandFolder}/browse_instagram_partnerships.json`, and summaries
</task>

<task id="browse-meta-ads" label="Step 4c: Browse Meta Ads Library">
## Browse Meta Ads Library by Brand/Competitor Names

Same flow as `instagram-research.anyt` Step 6 — use ONLY `competitor_keywords` (brand names) via `--advertiser`:

```bash
uv run --project skills/browse-meta-ads-library/runtime \
  skills/browse-meta-ads-library/runtime/browse.py \
  --advertiser "{client_name}" \
  --advertiser "{competitor 1}" \
  --advertiser "{competitor 2}" \
  ... \
  --country {targetCountry from @product-info} \
  -n 25 \
  -o {brandFolder}/ads_research.json
```

**Immediately** download creatives from each ad's `creative_urls` (signed CDN URLs expire in hours) into `{brandFolder}/thumbnails/ad-{slug}.jpg`.

### Finding the real advertisers inside the noise

Keyword search returns mostly unrelated brands, and the genuine competitor is
usually sitting in that noise under its **Facebook Page name** — the product
name plus a tagline, not the product name alone:

| You searched | The advertiser is actually called |
|---|---|
| `Alarmy` | `Alarmy - My Daily Success Habits` |
| `Opal` | `Opal・#1 Screen Time App` |
| `one sec` | `one sec app` |
| `AppBlock` | *(returned `Opal・#1 Screen Time App`)* |

Select on the **`advertiser` page name containing the product token**, not on ad
copy — unrelated advertisers use the same vocabulary ("focus", "screen time",
"block") and will pass a text filter. Searching a competitor's name also
surfaces *other* competitors: `WakeClock - Alarm Challenge` only appeared under
the `Alarmy` query and was in nobody's competitor list. Feed anything you find
this way back into the competitor set.

### Per-query validation (this step fails silently)

`browse.py` reports one aggregate count — `Search complete: 25 ads collected
across 6 query(ies)` — which looks like success even when five of the six
queries returned nothing. Check `summary.keyword_breakdown` per query:

```bash
python3 -c "import json;print({k:v['ad_count'] for k,v in json.load(open('{brandFolder}/ads_research.json'))['summary']['keyword_breakdown'].items()})"
```

A query at `0` while others return 17-25 is a **capture failure, not an absence
of ads**. Re-run those advertisers **one per invocation** (the scraper degrades
after the first query in a batch) and merge the results. Meta also rate-limits,
so a zero can persist for a genuinely-advertising brand across several attempts.

**Never conclude "nobody in this category advertises" from keyword search.** A
zero is only ever evidence about the capture, not about the market. Report the
ads you confirmed and say which advertisers you could not get a clean read on;
one run reported a whole category as running no paid social when Alarmy, Opal
and WakeClock were all live at the time.

**Output:** `{brandFolder}/ads_research.json`, `ads_research_top.md`, ad thumbnails
</task>

<task id="browse-youtube" label="Step 4d: Browse YouTube Shorts">
## Browse YouTube Shorts

Same flow as `youtube-shorts-research.anyt` Step 4 (headless, no login needed):

```bash
uv run --project skills/browse-youtube-shorts/runtime \
  skills/browse-youtube-shorts/runtime/browse.py \
  {product queries first, then theme queries; each in quotes} \
  -n 10 \
  --min-views 100000 \
  --time-range {timeRange mapped to today/week/month/year/all} \
  -o {brandFolder}/browse_youtube.json
```

Run a second pass with verified channel names plus product/model terms and write
`{brandFolder}/browse_youtube_brands.json`.

Run a third sweep from `keywords_youtube_partnerships.json`, sorted by views,
and write `{brandFolder}/browse_youtube_partnerships.json`. Include queries such
as the plain brand, compact brand hashtag, `{brand} review`, `{brand} demo`,
`{brand} tutorial`, `{brand} sponsored`, and `{brand} partner`. Inspect the
Short title and description for brand tags, disclosures, affiliate links/codes,
and calls to action. For shortlisted channels, inspect the public About text and
outbound links for the brand/domain or ambassador/partner claims before assigning
a relationship.

**Output:** `{brandFolder}/browse_youtube.json`,
`{brandFolder}/browse_youtube_brands.json`,
`{brandFolder}/browse_youtube_partnerships.json`, and summaries
</task>

### Brand reach top-up and two-pool audit

Do not use the first five official posts a browse happens to return. For each
organic platform:

Generate a repeatable gap/query plan before each top-up pass:

```bash
python3 skills/initial-social-content-research/runtime/discovery_policy.py \
  --data {brandFolder}/report_data.json \
  --profile {brandFolder}/product_profile.json \
  --competitors {brandFolder}/competitors_confirmed.json \
  --audit {brandFolder}/curation_audit.json \
  -o {brandFolder}/discovery_top_up_plan.json
```

The plan emits ordered `passes` for each platform and pool. Run them in order:

- Brand: `official-account`, `product-query`, `brand-hashtag`,
  `partnership-query`, `retailer-distributor`, `content-type-expansion`,
  `indexed-fallback`.
- Creator: `exact-product`, `competitor-product`, `product-decision`,
  `precise-problem`, `content-type-expansion`, `mined-hashtag`,
  `indexed-fallback`.

Each pool targets 12 relevant candidates for five cards, with four cards per
page as the delivery minimum. The planner automatically mines hashtags only
from specificity 2-3 candidates and emits up to three primary batches of eight
queries per mode, preserving the remaining queries as reserve batches so later
entities do not disappear behind a platform timeout. Run primary batches,
re-curate, and run reserve batches only while the page remains below four. After each batch,
merge and deduplicate candidates, apply the relevance gate, update
`report_data.json`, and regenerate the plan. Stop only when every page has at
least four qualified cards, continuing toward five from the candidate buffer.
A zero-result capture advances to the next batch and indexed fallback; it never
proves that relevant content does not exist. Query breadth, content-type lanes,
and relevant hashtags expand before creator engagement floors relax; relevance
and attribution never relax.

1. Verify official accounts/channels from the competitor research or the linked
   profile itself; record `official_account_verified: true` only with that proof.
2. Collect candidates through both `official-account` and `product-query` modes.
   Aim for 10+ relevant brand candidates across 3+ accounts. When coverage is
   thinner, record a `brand_gap_note` instead of inventing completeness.
3. If the selected official set is conspicuously low-reach, top up with product
   names, model names, `review`, `setup`, `comparison`, `fitting`, and `test`,
   including verified retailers or distributors for those same products.
4. Filter for relevance, then rank by the platform's real metric. The
   highest-reach eligible brand candidate must be selected. Any higher-reach
   candidate omitted for diversity, duplication, recency, or unusable media
   needs an explicit `exclusion_reason`.
5. Keep brand and creator pools separate through curation. A paid or disclosed
   brand partnership belongs in the brand pool; an independent review belongs
   in the creator pool.

<task id="curate-content" label="Step 5: Curate ~10 Contents per Platform">
## Select and Diversify the Content Set

From the browse results + competitor presence data, curate per platform:

- **5 brand/competitor contents** — a deliberate mix of official/owned posts and creator-side branded UGC, influencer/KOC/KOL partnerships, ambassador posts, or attributable product integrations (ideally across several brands)
- **5 organic creator contents** — non-brand creators winning in the niche
- **Delivery floor:** every brand and creator page must contain at least **4**
  relevant cards. Five is still the target. A page below four fails production
  validation instead of shipping as an empty or one-card page
- **Product relevance (hard, applied FIRST):** every selected card needs a
  supported relevance test, 2-3 specificity score, and concrete evidence. Rank
  on engagement only after filtering for relevance, never before
- **Minimum engagement (organic creators, adaptive):** creator content starts at
  **>=50K views/likes**. If targeted product/model, competitor/domain, use-case,
  compact-hashtag, and mined-hashtag top-ups still cannot fill five relevant
  slots, relax to **>=10K**. Use **>=1K** only after all top-up modes complete.
  If four relevant cards still cannot be filled, a no-minimum verified-niche
  fallback is the last resolution; record `creator_top_up_complete: true` and a
  concrete `creator_gap_note`. Never relax the relevance gate.
  **Brand/competitor content has no hard engagement
  floor** because useful owned posts may be small. Rank the relevant brand pool
  by reach, distinct format, and relationship confidence; when partnership UGC
  materially outperforms low-view official posts, show that distribution
  strategy instead of filling the page with weak owned examples
- **Diversity rules (hard):** max **3 videos from the same account** per platform;
  require **3+ distinct content types** across the platform and aim for **3+
  distinct content formats** (POV skit, talking head,
  street interview, UGC demo, meme, vlog, screen demo…)
- Label every video with `handle`, `metric` (likes/views), `format`, `source`,
  and the full `relevance` object below. For creator-side brand content also
  retain `relationship`, `relationship_confidence`, `relationship_evidence`,
  `creator_profile_evidence`, `promotion_strength`, `promotion_evidence`, and
  `discovery_query`

### Analyze likely creator collaborations

A caption is not the video. Before classifying shortlisted creator-side brand
content, run `trend-video-understanding` with the named brand:

```bash
uv run --project skills/trend-video-understanding/runtime \
  skills/trend-video-understanding/runtime/understand_video.py \
  --url "{permanent post URL}" \
  --brand "{brand or product name}" \
  --context "{client niche and why this post was shortlisted}" \
  -o {brandFolder}/video-analysis/brand-partnerships/{platform}-{post_id}.json
```

Use its evidence-based `promotion_strength` separately from relationship
evidence:

- **`none`** — the named product is absent; reject it from the brand set
- **`incidental`** — briefly visible or named but not material; normally reject
- **`integrated`** — central to the demo, story, problem, or outcome
- **`direct`** — explicitly recommends, sells, or asks viewers to use, buy, or
  download the product

Retain `promotion_evidence`, `call_to_action`, and
`creator_native_ugc_style`. UGC style describes the creative format; it is not
proof of payment. Inspect the creator profile separately and save the exact bio
excerpt, resolved brand URL/domain, stated ambassador/partner role, source URL,
and date checked. A generic creator bio, an unresolved link hub, or a brand
hashtag repeated only in the post is not independent profile evidence.

### Brand relationship gate

A creator post belongs in the brand/competitor set only when the brand-product
relationship is directly attributable. Classify it before ranking:

1. **`confirmed_paid`** — the platform shows a paid-partnership label, or the
   caption/title/description contains an explicit disclosure (`#ad`,
   `#sponsored`, `paid partnership`, `partnered with`, `Publicité`, or a local
   equivalent) or unambiguous sponsorship statement.
2. **`commercial_affiliate`** — an affiliate link, commission disclosure, promo
   code, or creator-specific landing page proves a commercial relationship but
   does not by itself prove a paid sponsorship. Label it `Affiliate creator
   integration` unless another disclosure also supports `confirmed_paid`.
3. **`potential_collaboration`** — no explicit disclosure exists, but the video
   is `direct` creator-native promotion, or `integrated` with at least two
   observable brand-forward signals, and independent profile evidence names the
   brand/domain or claims an ambassador/partner relationship. Set
   `relationship_confidence: medium`, label it `Potential creator collaboration`,
   and state that it is unconfirmed.
4. **`brand_attributed`** — the named product is shown or demonstrated and the
   creator directly tags the brand or uses its brand hashtag, but payment is not
   proven. Label the card `Creator product integration`, never `Sponsored`.
5. **`owned`** — the post comes from a verified official or founder account.

A brand hashtag alone is enough to enter discovery, not enough to confirm a
relationship. Analyze rather than discard it. Reject generic hashtag stuffing
when the product is absent or merely incidental. Do not infer KOC or KOL status
from views alone; use creator/follower metadata when available and otherwise say
`creator placement`. Keep caption, video-analysis, profile, and permanent-URL
evidence separate so every report label is auditable.

### The content-type taxonomy

Content types are a **cross-cutting tag, not a structure**. The body of the deck
stays organised BY PLATFORM (slides 5-11, brand/competitor then organic creator
for each — category context leads into adaptable formats) — that is how a reader navigates it. Tag every curated card with the
type it belongs to, then let the *distribution* of those tags drive three places
only: the **executive summary**, the **formats slide**, and the **strategy
section**. Never reorder or regroup the platform slides around types.

Most categories use four types:

1. **Branded / owned IP** — the brand's own channel, fronted by a recurring
   figure: a real person (founder, host) or a virtual/character IP. Highest
   trust, slowest to build, and the type most often left empty.
2. **Branded commercial** — TVC, paid social, product spots. Meta Ads is the
   read here; an empty Ad Library means the category competes organically.
3. **Educational** — the idea taught or dramatised. Suits story and animation,
   and the register follows the brand (ink-wash for a classics app, bright 2D
   for a kids product). This is where generation replaces hand-made scale.
4. **UGC / testimonial** — real users narrating their own journey with the
   product or the idea behind it. Cheapest to seed, hardest to fake.

Do not force content into four boxes when the category genuinely uses another:
add a type (creator code-drop, listicle placement, reaction/duet, comparison)
when the evidence shows one, and say why it earned a slot.

**Score each type, don't just name it.** For every type record: is anyone doing
it, what does the ceiling look like, and who holds it. A type nobody occupies at
any scale is an opportunity; a type one funded incumbent dominates is a fight.
That scoring is what makes the summary, formats slide and strategy pages
actionable rather than descriptive — one research run found three of four types
unoccupied, which changed the recommendation from "post more" to "run three
lanes in parallel". The platform slides carried exactly the same cards
throughout; only the framing around them changed.

### The relevance gate

Engagement ranking on its own fills the deck with the biggest broad-theme post.
Keep a video only when it passes one of these tests:

1. `exact-client-product` (specificity 3) — the client product/model is named,
   shown, demonstrated, reviewed, compared, fitted, set up, or serviced.
2. `named-competitor-product` (specificity 3) — the same proof exists for a
   confirmed competitor product/model.
3. `product-decision-service` (specificity 2 or 3) — the creator compares,
   chooses, sizes, fits, configures, maintains, repairs, or tests the exact kind
   of product the client sells. The product is the subject, not background gear.
4. `precise-product-problem` (specificity 2 or 3) — the caption, transcript, or
   on-screen action states and demonstrates a concrete problem the product
   solves.

Record the proof on both the candidate and selected report card:

```json
"relevance": {
  "test": "product-decision-service",
  "specificity": 2,
  "evidence": "The transcript compares 3/2 and 4/3 wetsuits by water temperature."
}
```

Reject anything that only shares a *technology, aesthetic, or topic* with the
product. Those posts often out-perform on-category ones by 3-5x, which is
exactly why an engagement-first pass keeps selecting them.

For physical products, visible category participation is not enough: skiing in
snow gear, surfing on a board, or filming an outdoor montage does not pass unless
the content makes a product/model, selection, setup, fit, maintenance, test, or
precise product problem central. For apps, a generic lifestyle problem does not
pass unless the app/product job is shown or explicitly discussed.

Observed failures worth recognising, all of which cleared the engagement floor
comfortably and told the client nothing:

| Product | Video that slipped through | Why it fails |
|---|---|---|
| AI character chat app | AI-generated crime drama, 301K likes | AI-made, but no character to talk to and no app |
| AI character chat app | "Usher but make it AI", 104K likes | shares the technology, not the job |
| AI character chat app | AI-video character-consistency tutorial, 90K likes | a filmmaking tip, not a consumer use case |
| Focus / anti-procrastination app | "how parents wake you up", 296K likes | matches the *persona*, but nothing is being focused or blocked |

If a platform cannot fill 5 relevant slots, **run the targeted sweep in Step 3
before lowering the bar**, and only then ship fewer cards with the gap
disclosed. A short on-category set beats a full off-category one.

Fetch thumbnails into `{brandFolder}/thumbnails/`:

```bash
# TikTok: oEmbed
curl -sL "https://www.tiktok.com/oembed?url={video_url}" | python3 -c "import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['thumbnail_url'])" | xargs -I{} curl -sL -o "{brandFolder}/thumbnails/tiktok-{video_id}.jpg" "{}"

# YouTube Shorts
yt-dlp --skip-download --write-thumbnail --convert-thumbnails jpg -o "{brandFolder}/thumbnails/yt-%(id)s.%(ext)s" {urls}

# Instagram: yt-dlp or the Reel page og:image
```

Verify: `find {brandFolder}/thumbnails -name '*.jpg' -size -15k -print` — re-capture anything under 15 KB.

Write `{brandFolder}/curation_audit.json` with, per platform:

```json
{
  "brand_search_modes": ["official-account", "product-query", "brand-hashtag",
    "partnership-query", "retailer-distributor", "content-type-expansion",
    "indexed-fallback"],
  "brand_candidates": [{"url": "…", "handle": "…", "metric": "…",
    "format": "…", "official_account_verified": true, "selected": true,
    "relevance": {"test": "named-competitor-product", "specificity": 3,
      "evidence": "…"}}],
  "creator_candidates": [{"url": "…", "handle": "…", "metric": "…",
    "format": "…", "selected": false, "exclusion_reason": "general topic",
    "relevance": {"test": "general-topic", "specificity": 1,
      "evidence": "Outdoor montage shows the activity but never identifies or evaluates a product."}}],
  "creator_floor": 50000,
  "creator_search_modes": ["exact-product", "competitor-product",
    "product-decision", "precise-problem", "content-type-expansion",
    "mined-hashtag", "indexed-fallback"],
  "brand_top_up_complete": false,
  "creator_top_up_complete": false,
  "brand_gap_note": "optional",
  "creator_gap_note": "optional"
}
```

Selected candidates use one of the four accepted relevance tests and specificity
2–3. Rejected candidates may instead use `general-topic`, `incidental-product`,
or `unverified-product` with specificity 0–1 so the audit preserves what was
screened out. Every rejected candidate needs `exclusion_reason`. A 10K creator
fallback needs `creator_gap_note`; a 1K fallback additionally requires
`creator_top_up_complete: true`. Fewer than 12 candidates, fewer than five
selections, or a creator floor below 50K requires every ordered mode in the
matching `*_search_modes`, a concrete gap note, and
`*_top_up_complete: true`. A zero creator floor is allowed only for verified,
relevant niche cards after the full 50K → 10K → 1K sequence and every search
mode has been exhausted.

**Output:** `curation_audit.json`, curated lists, and thumbnails
</task>

<break id="review-content" label="Review Curated Content (optional)">
Review the curated per-platform lists and thumbnails in `{brandFolder}/`. Swap out anything off-niche. Skip when running autonomously — the diversity validator in the report skill flags violations.
</break>

<task id="generate-report" label="Step 6: Generate Social Content Research Report">
## Build the Deck with the social-content-research-report Skill

1. Consolidate everything into `{brandFolder}/report_data.json` following the schema in `skills/social-content-research-report/SKILL.md`:
   - `cover` — client name, subtitle, `{preparedFor from @product-info}`, current month/year
   - `exec` — 3 findings + "what this means for content". Lead with the **content-type distribution** (which of the four types are occupied, by whom, at what ceiling) rather than a platform-by-platform recap; the reader already gets platforms on slides 5-11. State the opportunity as which types are open. Every finding carries a measured number.
   - `landscape` — tight copy + ONE hero stat (the most striking number found)
   - `competitive` — 3 tier rows from `competitors_confirmed.json` + presence data
   - `platforms.tiktok/instagram/youtube` — the curated 5+5 video lists from Step 5; every card retains the audit's `relevance` object. On creator-side brand cards preserve the working relationship and promotion fields; set `content_type` or `relationship_label` to `Potential creator collaboration` for supported unconfirmed cases, never `Sponsored`. Keep every `brand_intro` and `creator_intro` to one short insight (two sentences and 160 characters maximum). Distinguish relationship types only when that changes the takeaway; put counts, verification detail, rejected candidates, and search methodology in `curation_audit.json`, never in the slide intro.
   - `meta_ads` — 6 ads from `ads_research.json` (filter `advertiser` to real target brands); include each ad's `ad_id` so the deck cards deep-link to the specific ad (`https://www.facebook.com/ads/library/?id={ad_id}`), not a keyword search. Keep `intro` to one short insight (two sentences and 160 characters maximum); query coverage and rejected advertisers stay in `curation_audit.json`.
   - `formats` — the **four content types**, one card each, tagged `1 · BRANDED IP` / `2 · COMMERCIAL` / `3 · EDUCATIONAL` / `4 · UGC` (swap or add a type when the category uses a different one). Each description says who occupies the type and its measured ceiling, so the slide reads as a map of open ground rather than a list of styles.
   - `connect` — About Adant AI product overview: keep the capability-card defaults, use `contact@anyloop.ai`, and include case-study identities, URLs, or performance metrics only when they are verified public information or explicitly approved by the user
   - `strategies` — the Sample Content Strategy section (see the sub-step below)

   As soon as the research fields validate, emit `report_data.json` as a
   Sidecar artifact and build a distinct 13-slide `research_preview.html` plus
   markdown copy. Emit the HTML preview too, so the first human-readable report
   appears before the bounded strategy analyses and final rendering continue.

2. **Sample Content Strategy** — rank the **top 8 inspiration videos** out of
everything curated in Step 5: five primary picks and three reserves. Launch at
most two primary analyses concurrently, launching the next pick when one active
job finishes. In Codex Browser mode, save each clip
through the authenticated browser and pass `--video <local-path> --url
<permanent-platform-url>` without inspecting cookies or storage; a DOM `blob:`
URL is not itself downloadable. On other hosts use the hardened `yt-dlp` path.
On a structured `download_failed` or phase timeout, switch acquisition backend
once, then promote the next ranked reserve rather than rerunning the same
command in the foreground. Stop when five analyses succeed and turn those into
copy-paste Adant messages.

   **Cover the types, not just the platforms.** Distribute the 5 strategies across the content types the evidence supports, weighted to where the client can realistically win — a type nobody occupies deserves a strategy even at a low view count, because the ceiling is a statement about competition, not about demand. Title each strategy with its type (`Type 1 · …`) so the section reads as a plan. State plainly when a type has no strategy and why (no evidence, or the client cannot produce it).

   Selection: every candidate must already have passed the curation relevance gate — strategies come only from curated cards, so an off-category card becomes an off-category strategy. Within that pool rank by engagement, then by how cleanly the hook maps onto this product, then by format diversity. Hard rules: **max one video per account**, **at least two platforms represented**, no video that already appears twice in the deck, and organic creator picks still obey the engagement floor. Prefer videos whose format the product can actually stage (a screen demo beats a stunt the product cannot reproduce).

   Sanity-check each pick by writing its `what_to_change` line first: if swapping the product in requires changing what the video *is about* rather than what it shows, the pick is off-category no matter how well it performed.

   Read `skills/social-content-strategist/SKILL.md` before writing these — the block shape below is that skill's, and the deck renders the same **General Instructions** verbatim from `content-strategy-generator`, once, on the section opener. Per strategy, write only:

   ```text
   analyze <inspiration url>

   Avatar: <TYPE> — <specific look>

   Keep: <what carries over, and why>

   Change: <how the product swaps in>

   Overlay:
   <2-3 short lines, last one the product tag>
   ```

   One idea per line and the url alone on the first, so a long permalink can
   never push another field onto a wrapped second line.

   ### Derive the avatar from the reference, never from habit

   **Watch the video before writing the avatar.** Run `trend-video-understanding`
   on every strategy pick and read `fingerprint.character`, `setting` and the
   narrative pass's visual-style notes. Defaulting to "a UGC avatar" is the most
   common failure here: it puts a phone-shot person in front of an animated
   reference, a studio commercial, or a piece with no character at all.
   Prefer its `--video` seam when the authenticated browser already downloaded
   a clip; otherwise pass the dedicated research-profile cookie spec only when
   that profile was used for discovery. Never inspect or print cookie values.
   Only `status: ok` is cached, so a failed acquisition remains retryable.

   Name a TYPE and then the specific look:

   | Type | When the reference is | Say something like |
   |---|---|---|
   | **UGC** | phone-shot, handheld, one real person | `UGC — early-20s student at a desk, handheld, no makeup` |
   | **Animation** | drawn or rendered — always name the style | `Animation (2D ink-wash)`, `Animation (3D Pixar-style)`, `Animation (anime)`, `Animation (claymation)` |
   | **Commercial / TVC** | lit set, polished grade, product hero | `Commercial — studio-lit presenter, shallow depth of field` |
   | **Drama / cinematic** | acted scene, film grade, blocking | `Cinematic actor — two-hander in a kitchen, warm film grade` |
   | **Narrator only** | voice over b-roll or screen capture | `No on-camera person — screen recording with a calm voiceover` |
   | **Product only** | the object is the subject | `No performer — product on a plain sweep, macro moves` |

   Animation without a named style is not enough: `3d`, `anime`, `2d`, `Pixar`,
   `claymation`, `paper-cut`, `ink-wash` all read completely differently, and the
   right one usually follows the brand rather than the reference.

   ### Pick the reuse axis per video — it is not always the hook

   `Keep:` names WHAT is being carried over. Choose from the fingerprint's
   `hook_type`, `viral_format`, `why_it_works` and the narrative visual-style
   notes, and say it plainly:

   - **the hook** — the opening beat is the whole reason it worked; reproduce it near-verbatim
   - **the viral format** — the mechanic travels (list, POV, duet, before/after, reveal) while the opening can be rewritten
   - **the visual style** — the look is the draw; borrow the grade, palette, typography and pacing but tell a different story
   - **the structure** — the beat map and length carry it, not any single line
   - **the pacing / edit rhythm** — cut cadence and sound design are the signature
   - **the format, inverted** — same mechanic, opposite outcome, when a straight clone would read as a copy

   Say which, and why this video earns that axis. A deck where all five say
   "keep the hook" has not looked at the videos.

   No full scripts, no timelines, no per-strategy repetition of the general
   instructions. The avatar is a suggestion only — never generate an image for
   it. Each entry goes into `report_data.json` as:

   ```json
   {"strategies": {
     "headline": "…", "intro": "…", "closingHeadline": "…", "closingCopy": "…",
     "items": [{"title": "…", "platform": "tiktok", "format": "POV SKIT",
                "metric": "1.2M likes", "posted": "YYYY-MM-DD", "handle": "@x",
                "url": "https://…", "thumb": "thumbnails/tiktok-1.jpg",
                "why_this_video": "…", "avatar": "Animation (2D ink-wash) — …",
                "keep": "the viral format — …", "change": "…",
                "style": "optional extra art-direction line",
                "overlays": ["…", "…", "Product: tagline"]}]}}
   ```

   `keep` / `change` supersede `hook_to_keep` / `what_to_change`; the renderer
   still accepts the old keys so existing report data keeps building.

   Fewer than 5 qualifying videos: ship what qualifies and say so in the delivery note rather than padding with weak picks. Omit `strategies` entirely and the deck falls back to 13 slides. In `fast-draft` mode, omit strategy work by contract and label the 13-slide result as an incomplete diagnostic; do not run the production validator or AdAnt handoff.

3. Validate the selected cards against the full candidate audit:

```bash
python3 skills/initial-social-content-research/runtime/validate_curation.py \
  --data {brandFolder}/report_data.json \
  --audit {brandFolder}/curation_audit.json \
  --require-min-cards 4 \
  --require-type-coverage
```

For a full five-card delivery, append `--require-full-cards`; the validator then
requires exactly five brand and five creator cards on TikTok, Instagram, and
YouTube.

The validator rejects generic-topic creator cards, missing relevance evidence,
brand posts that are neither verified official nor supported by relationship
and promotion evidence, an omitted highest-reach eligible brand post,
unexplained rejections, insufficient search modes, engagement-floor violations,
and report/audit drift. Fix the research or disclose a real gap; do not weaken
the validator to fill a slide.

4. Build HTML + markdown (validates diversity rules + unfilled placeholders). Final report files are named `{product_name}_social_content_research_{date}` (lowercase product slug from `brand_folder`, date as `YYYY-MM-DD`):

```bash
uv run --project skills/social-content-research-report/runtime \
  skills/social-content-research-report/runtime/build_deck.py \
  --data {brandFolder}/report_data.json \
  -o {brandFolder}/{product_name}_social_content_research_{date}.html \
  --md {brandFolder}/{product_name}_social_content_research_{date}.md
```

5. Render PDF:

```bash
python3 skills/slide-pdf-generator/runtime/to_pdf.py \
  {brandFolder}/{product_name}_social_content_research_{date}.html \
  {brandFolder}/{product_name}_social_content_research_{date}.pdf --wait 8
```

6. Check: exactly 20 pages (13 research + strategy opener + 5 strategies + closing), no gray thumbnail boxes, no overflow clipping, no pricing/deliverables anywhere. Every potential-collaboration card must have a saved promotion analysis plus independent profile evidence and must remain explicitly unconfirmed. Every strategy message must name a URL that also appears on a research slide. Run `build_deck.py --strict`: video-card and ads intros are one short insight, at most two sentences and 160 characters, and never contain rejected candidates, query coverage, blank-caption notes, missing-handle notes, or other audit methodology.

**Output:** `{brandFolder}/{product_name}_social_content_research_{date}.pdf` + `.md` (+ `.html` clickable review copy)
</task>

